# EXAMPLE production drop-in — NOT INSTALLED, and deliberately NOT the live file.
#
# Copy to /etc/systemd/system/automation-suite-followups.service.d/20-preparation-live.conf
# (drop the .example suffix) when you decide to turn preparation on.
#
# WHY THIS LIVES IN A DROP-IN RATHER THAN THE UNIT IN GIT
#
# The base unit ships every one of these OFF. Two of them commit real-world resources:
#   * ALLOW_PAID_LLM_CALLS  -> spends money at the model provider
#   * FOLLOWUP_PREPARATION_ENABLED -> is what makes those calls happen
# Version control should never carry "spend money" as a default, for the same reason it never
# carries "send email" as a default. Keeping them here makes enabling them an explicit, dated,
# auditable act on one machine instead of a property of the repository.
#
# It also scopes the blast radius. ALLOW_PAID_LLM_CALLS is a GLOBAL paid-call kill switch consulted
# by the audit, composer, and email services alike. Setting it in this unit's environment affects
# ONLY this unit's process. Putting it in /home/opc/automation-suite/.env would instead arm paid
# calls for every CLI invocation on the box, including ad-hoc manual ones and anything the
# scheduled-send unit shells out to. Keep it here, never in .env.
#
# systemd applies drop-ins AFTER the main unit file, and for Environment= the last assignment of a
# given variable wins — so these override the base unit's false values. VERIFY the resolved result
# on the box rather than trusting that from memory:
#     systemctl show -p Environment automation-suite-followups.service
#
# LLM_PROVIDER=openai IS REQUIRED HERE, NOT OPTIONAL.
#
# ALLOW_PAID_LLM_CALLS only PERMITS spending; it does not SELECT a provider, and LLM_PROVIDER
# defaults to `mock`. A box armed for production but left on that default would compose FIXTURE copy
# and persist it into the human review queue as a real draft for a real prospect — text that reads
# like reviewable copy but describes nothing. Unattended preparation therefore refuses to run unless
# LLM_PROVIDER=openai (or FOLLOWUP_PREPARATION_ALLOW_MOCK_LLM=true is set deliberately), and fails
# the unit BEFORE listing a single candidate. Selecting the provider here rather than in .env keeps
# the paid provider scoped to this one unit, exactly like the paid-call switch above.
#
# THE MODELS ARE DELIBERATELY NOT PINNED HERE. EMAIL_WRITER_MODEL / EMAIL_REVIEWER_MODEL belong in
# validated .env configuration: they are not an authority switch, they are already hard-gated (an
# unknown or misspelled model has no verified price and throws before any paid call), and pinning
# them here would split model configuration across two places — so an ad-hoc `outreach-compose-preview`
# would silently use a different model than the one that writes the copy an operator reviews. This
# drop-in carries AUTHORITY (which provider, whether spending is allowed, which phases run); .env
# carries validated configuration.
#
# FOLLOWUP_PROGRESSION_ENABLED stays FALSE here on purpose. Preparation only composes copy and parks
# it in the human review queue. Progression is what advances approved copy toward a send schedule,
# and the first real follow-up must stop after Gmail DRAFT creation for manual thread inspection —
# so progression is run BY HAND, one stage at a time, until threading is verified. See
# "Controlled first-follow-up validation" in docs/AUTOMATION_PILOT.md.

[Service]
Environment=FOLLOWUP_PREPARATION_ENABLED=true
Environment=FOLLOWUP_PROGRESSION_ENABLED=false
Environment=ALLOW_PAID_LLM_CALLS=true
Environment=LLM_PROVIDER=openai
